clusion:**\nThe driver is present, seated upright with hands on the steering wheel in a standard driving position. While he appears ready to intervene, the system status shown in Frame 3 confirms the truck is in autonomous mode. No physical toggle switches with legible labels are visible on the dashboard; the only clear text is on the digital overlay indicating the autonomous system is engaged. The driver is monitoring, not actively driving.\n'], 'condensed_response': '**Scene Summary (Frames\u202f1345‑1370)**  \n\n- **Driver:** A bearded man in a dark T‑shirt, baseball cap and sunglasses is seated upright in the truck’s driver’s seat (visible in Frame\u202f2). His hands are on the wheel at roughly the 10\u202fo’clock (left) and 2\u202fo’clock (right) positions – the typical “hand‑over‑hand/9‑and‑3” posture required for supervised autonomy.  \n\n- **Dashboard:** The cabin shows a modern truck console with air‑vents, but no clearly legible toggle switches or labeled buttons can be seen.  \n\n- **System Overlay (Frame\u202f3):** A heads‑up display overlays the interior, reading:  \n  - **“EMBARK”** – system name  \n  - **“Engaged”** with a green indicator  \n  - **Timer\u202f00:27:02** and **Speed\u202f73\u202fMPH**  \n  - Purple/blue lane‑line graphics indicating a planned path.  \n\n- **Interaction Mode:** The driver’s posture suggests readiness to intervene, yet the HUD explicitly states the vehicle is in **autonomous mode**. Therefore the driver is **monitoring** the system rather than actively controlling the truck.  \n\n- **Conclusion:** The scene shows a driver present and attentive, hands on the wheel for safety compliance, while the truck operates autonomously as confirmed by the HUD overlay; no physical dashboard toggles are visible.', 'failed_images': []}}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Looking at the VLM analysis, I can see that frame 1350 shows the first driver sitting in a truck cabin, and frame 1355 shows the overlay with the "Engaged" toggle switch